  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: aef08fe7bbe81412eaacd58c23b63316, type: 3}
  m_Name: phoenix_claw
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: phoenix_claw
  Title: The Claw of the Phoenix
  Author: Anonymous
  Color: {r: 0.95, g: 0.55, b: 0.25, a: 1}
  Hint: High above the spire, on the sky island
  Pages:
  - 'High above the clouds, on an island that floats where the spire points, there
    lived a phoenix who had grown tired of burning.


    Every hundred years she
    flared into flame, crumbled into ash, and hatched again, small and new and remembering
    nothing at all.


    "It''s the forgetting I mind," she told the clouds. "I
    never get to finish anything."'
  - 'Visitors climbed the spire now and then. They came with nets and spears and
    polite smiles, and they all wanted the same thing: a claw of the phoenix, which
    every old book says will brew a potion to make you live forever.


    The phoenix
    sent them back down rather faster than they had come up, and generally a little
    singed.'
  - 'Then one spring a witch climbed up who was not like the others.


    She was
    old and round and out of breath. On her back she carried a cauldron, a kettle,
    a picnic blanket and a bag so full of books that the strap had been mended four
    times.


    She sat down heavily on a rock and said, "Good afternoon. I''ve
    come for a claw of the phoenix."'
  - 'The phoenix ruffled up, ready to scorch. "For your potion of living forever,
    I suppose."


    "Good heavens, no," said the witch. "Forever sounds exhausting.
    I want it for a bookmark. I''m a slow reader, and I keep losing my place."


    The
    phoenix laughed so hard she very nearly caught fire eighty years early.'
  - 'Claws are not for giving away, even to bookish witches. So instead the phoenix
    plucked a single golden feather from her breast, curved and pointed exactly like
    a claw, and dropped it into the witch''s lap.


    "Thank you," said the witch,
    whose name was Agnes. "Now. Would you like to hear what happens in chapter one?"'
  - 'Agnes put the kettle on, opened the first book, and began to read.


    She
    came back the next evening, and the next. She read adventures with pirates in
    them, and love stories where everyone was far too proud to say anything, and
    one truly terrible cookbook that made them both cry with laughter.


    And
    always, when the light went, she marked their place with the golden feather.'
  - 'Years went by like this, one chapter at a time.


    Agnes climbed the spire
    a little more slowly every spring. Then one autumn she didn''t climb it at all.
    A letter came up instead, carried by a very puzzled pigeon.


    Inside was
    the golden feather, tucked into the book they had been reading, about halfway
    through.'
  - '"My knees have finally won," Agnes had written. "You''ll have to finish this
    one on your own. I''ve marked our place. Don''t you dare skip ahead."


    The
    phoenix did not know how to read. But she had a great deal of time, and she had
    listened to Agnes for twenty years, and slowly, word by word, she worked it out.'
  - 'When the hundredth year came, the phoenix burned. She flared up bright as morning,
    crumbled into ash, and hatched again, small and new.


    But this time, beside
    her nest, there lay a book with a golden feather in it. She opened it at the
    marked page, and she remembered. Not everything. But enough.


    She remembered
    Agnes.'
  - 'Some say the phoenix still lives on the sky island, reading through the long
    afternoons, and that she has started writing books of her own.


    This one,
    you may have noticed, was never signed.


    But if you look closely at its
    cover, there is a small scorch mark in one corner, shaped exactly like a claw.'
